Add one middleware in `app.js`, registered first, before `express.json()` and `sessionUser`, so malformed-body 400s and unauthenticated 401s are logged too. It writes one JSON line per request when the response closes:

```js
function requestLog(req, res, next) {
  const start = process.hrtime.bigint();
  res.on("close", () => {
    console.log(JSON.stringify({
      method: req.method,
      route: req.baseUrl + (req.route ? req.route.path : ""),
      status: res.statusCode,
      completed: res.writableFinished,
      ms: Number(process.hrtime.bigint() - start) / 1e6,
      userId: req.user && req.user.id,
      accountId: req.user && req.user.accountId,
    }));
  });
  next();
}

app.use(requestLog);
```

- **Event:** It uses `close` rather than `finish` so requests where the client disconnects are logged as well. `completed` marks those, because `statusCode` defaults to 200 when nothing was written.
- **Route:** It logs the route pattern (`/orders/:id/cancel`) instead of `req.originalUrl`, so ids don't create a separate value for every request. Unmatched requests have no `req.route` and log just the base path.
- **User fields:** `req.user` is read at close time, so it is populated for authenticated requests and absent on 401s.
- **Never log:** Request bodies (`billing_email` is personal data) or the `Cookie` header, which holds the session token.

I haven't run this, and Express isn't installed here. Which logging stack do you already use? If it is `pino` or similar, `pino-http` replaces the hand-written middleware, and the fields above map onto its serializers.

